      "body": "### Project URL\n\nhttps://github.com/sjh9714/mergewarden\n\n### Category\n\nJS\n\n### Project Title\n\nA checkout-free GitHub Action that checks whether an AI agent's pull request stayed inside the boundaries it declared\n\n### Project Description\n\nCoding agents open pull requests all day. MergeWarden checks each one against boundaries only your repository can define: did the PR stay inside its declared scope, did it edit agent instruction files, did it wire untrusted text into an agent prompt?\n\n### Highlights\n\n**It is deterministic — no LLM is called during analysis.** Every finding carries a rule ID, a severity, the exact path, canonical evidence, and a stable finding ID, and the whole decision can be replayed locally with `mergewarden replay`. Two runs on the same commit always produce the same answer, which is what makes it usable as a merge gate.\n\n**It never checks out or executes pull-request code.** It reads the PR through the GitHub API only, and loads its policy from the exact base commit — so a pull request cannot weaken the policy that judges it by editing the config in its own branch. There is no checkout step in the workflow.\n\n**It checks a layer that other tools do not.** Workflow linters like zizmor check workflow correctness; LLM reviewers give semantic opinions. MergeWarden asks a different question: did this pull request cross a boundary specific to *this* repository — the scope the agent declared, the `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` / `.mcp.json` files that steer every future agent run, or a path from a PR comment into an agentic workflow's prompt. The three are complementary.\n\n**The design came from measurement, not intuition.** We scanned 2,204 recently merged AI-agent pull requests on public repositories. Of the 349 that touched workflows or package manifests, 12.9% escalated workflow permissions and 17.5% introduced unpinned actions; 3.9% of all of them edited agent control-plane files. The default rules and the default detection lists are those findings, and every number reproduces from published queries.\n\nZero runtime dependencies, MIT licensed, TypeScript.\n\n### Example Code\n\n```markdown\nTry it with one command — no token, no repository, no network. It analyzes an example pull request bundled inside the CLI, using the default policy:\n\n\nnpx --yes mergewarden@0.5.0 demo\n\n\nScan a real public pull request:\n\n\nnpx --yes mergewarden@0.5.0 scan owner/repository#123\n\n\nInstall as a GitHub Action — note there is no `actions/checkout` step:\n\n\nname: MergeWarden\non:\n  pull_request:\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n  pull-requests: read\njobs:\n  mergewarden:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: sjh9714/mergewarden@v0.5.0\n        with:\n          github-token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n          mode: warn\n\n\nAn agent declares its intended scope in the pull-request body, and edits outside it become findings:\n\n\n<!-- mergewarden-contract\nversion: 1\nagent: codex\ntask: update session expiry handling\nallowed_paths:\n  - src/auth/**\n  - test/auth/**\n-->\n```\n\n### Screenshots or Demo Videos\n\nOne command, no token and no repository — this is a real `npx` run, not simulated output:\n\n![MergeWarden demo output](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sjh9714/mergewarden/main/docs/assets/mergewarden-demo-head.png)\n\nThe same command running (the full report is 95 lines, so it scrolls):\n\n![MergeWarden demo](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sjh9714/mergewarden/main/docs/assets/mergewarden-demo.gif)\n\nThe live pull request and Action run behind that report are public and can be inspected directly: https://github.com/sjh9714/agent-gate-install-smoke-20260617/pull/17\n\nA Simplified Chinese README is available at https://github.com/sjh9714/mergewarden/blob/main/README.zh-CN.md\n",
